---
description: New use cases must not disturb processes that already work
alwaysApply: true
---

# Build on existing flows

When adding a use case to a process that already works, keep that process intact.

## Do

- Add the new path beside the current one. Purchase booking, sales booking, lifts, and godown stock stay separate unless the user asks to change one of them.
- Reuse the existing document for the job it already does. A new quantity pool, label, or screen must not rewrite an open purchase, a booked sales order, or a lift that was already correct.
- Check the neighboring registers after the change: purchase order, sales order, lift, and inventory. Numbers and links that were right before must stay right.
- If the change is drastic, stop and say so before building. Name which working path it would change, and wait for the user to confirm.

## Do not

- Fold a new case into an old calculation so both cases share one balance, one ref, or one link.
- Relabel or rebook an existing order because a later step looks similar (a closed purchase, a lot number, a stock lift).
- “Simplify” by changing a working path the user did not ask to change.
